[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:What Every Writer Wants]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.87]Lesson 39 | 第39课
[00:03.71]What every writer wants | 作家之所需
[00:12.95]How do professional writers ignore what they were taught at school about writing? | 职业作家是如何无视他们在学校里学的写作知识的？
[00:21.57]I have known very few writers, | 我认识的作家很少，
[00:24.56]but those I have known and whom I respect, confess at once that they have little idea where they are going when they first set pen to paper. | 但我所认识并且敬重的作家都立即承认，他们提笔时对自己要写到哪里去几乎一无所知。
[00:36.13]They have a character, perhaps two; | 他们心中有一个人物，也许两个人；
[00:39.10]they are in that condition of eager discomfort which passes for inspiration all admit radical changes of destination once the journey has begun; | 他们处于那种急切不安的状态之中，这种状态被当作灵感；他们都承认，旅程一旦开始，目的地就会发生根本的改变；
[00:50.66]one, to my certain knowledge, spent nine months on a novel about Kashmir, | 据我确切所知，有一位作家花九个月写了一部关于克什米尔的小说，
[00:57.61]then reset the whole thing in the Scottish Highlands. | 然后把整个故事改设在了苏格兰高地。
[01:01.38]I never heard of anyone making a 'skeleton', as we were taught at school. | 我从未听说有谁像我们在学校里学的那样先拟“提纲”。
[01:07.35]In the breaking and remaking, in the timing interweaving, beginning afresh, | 在不断的打破与重写、时机的交织穿插、重新开始之中，
[01:14.80]the writer comes to discern things in his material which were not consciously in his mind when he began. | 作家开始辨别出素材中的东西，而这些东西在他动笔时并未有意识地想到。
[01:22.83]This organic process, often leading to moments of extraordinary self-discovery, is of an indescribable fascination. | 这种有机的过程，往往导致非凡的自我发现的时刻，具有难以形容的魅力。
[01:34.32]A blurred image appears; he adds a brushstroke and another and it is gone | 一个模糊的形象出现了；他添上一笔，又添一笔，它又不见了；
[01:41.06]but something was there, and he will not rest till he has captured it. | 但那里曾有某种东西，他捉不住它便不会罢休。
[01:46.68]Sometimes the yeast within a writer outlives a book he has written. | 有时，作家内心的酵母比他写成的书更长寿。
[01:52.77]I have heard of writers who read nothing but their own books; | 我听说有些作家除了自己的书什么都不读；
[01:57.01]like adolescents they stand before the mirror, | 他们像青少年一样站在镜子前，
[02:00.46]and still cannot fathom the exact outline of the vision before them. | 仍然捉摸不透眼前那个影子的确切轮廓。
[02:06.15]For the same reason, writers talk interminably about their own books, | 出于同样的原因，作家没完没了地谈论自己的书，
[02:12.37]winkling out hidden meanings, super-imposing new ones, | 挖掘隐藏的含义，强加上新的含义，
[02:17.19]begging response from those around them. | 恳求周围的人作出反应。
[02:20.57]Of course a writer doing this is misunderstood: he might as well try to explain a crime or a love affair. | 作家这样做当然会被误解：他这样做就像试图解释一桩罪行或一段恋情一样徒劳。
[02:28.15]He is also, incidentally, an unforgivable bore. | 顺便说一句，他也是一个不可原谅的令人厌烦的人。
[02:34.63]This temptation to cover the distance between himself and the reader, | 想要消除自己与读者之间的距离，
[02:39.63]to study his image in the sight of those who do not know him, can be his undoing: he has begun to write to please. | 想在那些不认识他的人眼中研究自己形象的这种诱惑，可能是他的毁灭：他已经开始为取悦他人而写作了。
[02:51.65]A young English writer made the pertinent observation a year or two back | 一两年前，一位年轻的英国作家作过中肯的评论：
[02:56.85]that the talent goes into the first draft, and the art into the drafts that follow. | 说天才放进初稿，而艺术放进随后的历次修改。
[03:03.60]For this reason also the writer, like any other artist, | 出于同样的原因，作家像任何其他艺术家一样，
[03:08.09]has no resting place, no crowd or movement in which he may take comfort, | 没有安息之所，没有可以从中获得安慰的人群或运动，
[03:15.18]no judgment from outside which can replace the judgment from within. | 没有来自外部的评判能取代来自内部的评判。
[03:20.66]A writer makes order out of the anarchy of his heart; | 作家用心中纷乱的激情创造出秩序；
[03:24.70]he submits himself to a more ruthless discipline than any critic dreamed of, | 他服从于一种比任何评论家所梦想的都更无情的纪律，
[03:30.73]and when he flirts with fame, he is taking time off from living with himself, | 而当他追逐名声时，他是在暂时逃避与自己相处，
[03:36.70]from the search for what his world contains at its inmost point. | 暂时逃避探索他的世界在最深处所蕴含的东西。
